	{
		"ContentDetails": {
			"VideoId": "d5im04T6AqA",
			"VideoPublishedAtRaw": "2025-08-15T01:50:37Z",
			"VideoPublishedAtDateTimeOffset": "2025-08-15T01:50:37+00:00",
			"VideoPublishedAt": "2025-08-15T07:20:37+05:30"
		},
		"ETag": "PB4chB7ZWxdxouktAssl_ZfMctc",
		"Id": "UEwxemdOQ29XdF83Yk9lR3VEYVZDYnd0UWlLcjF2ZGRFOS41NkI0NEY2RDEwNTU3Q0M2",
		"Kind": "youtube#playlistItem",
		"Snippet": {
			"ChannelId": "UCiZeuI-7OPs7TmMel6Q5Msw",
			"ChannelTitle": "Kanishk Nishar",
			"Description": "Well, this is Scherchen, so I won\u2019t bother expressing astonishment. But let\u2019s point out some really good things about this performance, Scherchen\u2019s first American appearance. We get to hear Gil Johnson, who had retired and been replaced by Frank Kaderabek by the time RCA commercially recorded Philadelphia in the Mahler 5 in 1977.  (Many consider Kaderabek\u2019s characteristic timbre a demerit in that performance.) We get to hear Philly rising to the insane tempo demands \u2014 careening from largo to prestissimo and back again, none provided for in the score \u2014 of a conductor with an idiosyncratic view of how the M5 should sound.  We get to hear the tam tams in the first movement, loud and proud. Philadelphia has commercially recorded in house the M5 under Eschenbach and Yannick, both downloadable. Of the three available commercial issues, I personally think Yannick\u2019s edges out the others. He pushes the PO in the scherzo in particular, thank goodness not to the cartoon proportions here, and boy do the players deliver.",
			"PlaylistId": "PL1zgNCoWt_7bOeGuDaVCbwtQiKr1vddE9",
			"Position": 7,
			"PublishedAtRaw": "2025-08-21T04:10:12Z",
